llama: the per-decode host timers, and the token embedding rows from the host

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9. The files of this patch are not touched by the other patches of this
repository.

Files:
    src/llama-context.h              the hostprof data and the embedding lookup buffer
    src/llama-context.cpp            the timers of process_ubatch and decode, the embedding lookup, the state timers
    tools/completion/completion.cpp  the time of the sampler for each token
    examples/simple/simple.cpp       the time of the sampler for each token

How to apply, from the llama.cpp root:

    git apply patches/hexagon-host/0002-llama-host-timers-and-embd-lookup.patch
    cmake --build build-snapdragon -j

Only libllama.so, llama-completion (libllama-completion-impl.so) and llama-simple change.

Switches (environment variables):

    LLAMA_HOSTPROF=1           print one line for each decode call, one line for each sampler call, and one
                               line for each sequence state get or set
    LLAMA_EMBD_LOOKUP_HOST=1   the token embedding rows come from the host

The decode line

    hostprof: decode #12 tokens 1 ubatches 1 reused 1 embd_host 0 | prologue 60 apply 20 reuse_check 15 build 0
              alloc 0 inputs 40 compute 1100 logits_get 25300 loop_other 10 epilogue 15 total 26560 | since_last_return 2900 us

The fields are microseconds. reused is the count of ubatches that reused the previous graph, build and
alloc are 0 then. compute is the time of the scheduler compute call (the backends queue their work in
it). logits_get is the copy of the logits to the host, and for the Hexagon backend the wait for the DSP is
in it. since_last_return is the time from the return of the previous decode call, thus the time of the
caller: the sampler and the loop. The sampler line is "hostprof: sample #N us T".

The graph reuse of llama.cpp (llm_graph_result::can_reuse) fires for a decode of one token when the
ubatch shape, the number of outputs, the KV length (n_kv, padded to 256) and the recurrent slot (head,
rs_z) stay the same. The perf print at the end of a run gives the count: "graphs reused". With the reuse,
the scheduler does not split the graph again, thus the split graphs keep their uid, and the Hexagon
backend finds its op nodes and its packed batch by that uid.

The token embedding rows from the host

The token embedding table is on the CPU. Thus the GET_ROWS of the tokens is a split of one node on the
CPU before the HTP0 split, and the scheduler copies its output to the device with the other inputs. With
LLAMA_EMBD_LOOKUP_HOST=1, decode reads the rows of the tokens on the host with the same dequantization
function that the CPU GET_ROWS op calls (ggml_get_type_traits(type)->to_float, and a memcpy for F32), and
gives the batch the rows as embeddings (batch.embd) in place of the tokens. The graph then takes the
inp_embd input, has no GET_ROWS node, and has one split. The rows are the same bytes, thus the graph
computes the same values, and the token stream is the same.

The lookup does not run, and the tokens stay, when the context is an MTP context, the model is DFLASH, a
LoRA adapter is active (the token path applies the adapter on the rows), the model has a deepstack layer
(build_inp_embd applies the embedding scale to the token path only), the table is not in a host buffer, or
the type has no to_float function.

The GET_ROWS node of the tokens stays in the graph without the compute flag, thus the scheduler still
makes a split for it on the CPU. Patch 0001 makes the scheduler skip a split with no computable node,
thus the two patches together remove the split and its copies. Without patch 0001 this patch removes the
compute of the node but not the split.
